e columns you need.**\n- `SELECT *` returns every column from both tables. That wastes I/O and breaks covering-index use.\n- If `orders` and `users` share column names such as `id` or `created_at`, `SELECT *` returns duplicates. Most clients then silently overwrite one with the other.\n- The query will also change shape whenever someone adds a column.\n\n**2. Don't wrap the indexed column in `lower()`.**\n- `lower(u.email)` can't use a plain index on `email`, so the database may scan the whole `users` table.\n- Better options, depending on your database:\n  - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. Keep the `lower()` in the query.\n  - Use a case-insensitive type or collation, such as `citext` in Postgres or a `_ci` collation in MySQL. Then you can write `u.email = 'a@example.org'`.\n  - Store emails already normalized to lowercase, and compare directly.\n\n**3. Make sure the join column is indexed.** `orders.user_id` should have an index. Foreign keys get one automatically in some databases (MySQL/InnoDB) but not in others (Postgres).\n\n**4. Style: use readable aliases and an explicit `ORDER BY` or `LIMIT` if you need them.** Without `ORDER BY`, the row order is undefined. If you only need recent orders, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`.\n\nA revised version, with column names that are guesses until you tell me your schema:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'   -- or plain `=` with citext/_ci collation\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC;\n```\n\nTell me which database you use and the table definitions. I can then tailor the column list and index statements, and edit `query.sql` directly if you'd like.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}